        type: "intro",
        title: "Thirteen Birds, One Big Idea",
        content: "When Darwin stepped onto the Galápagos Islands in September 1835, he collected birds he thought were a confusing mix of finches, wrens, blackbirds, and grosbeaks. He labeled them poorly and barely noted which island each came from. It was only back in London, when ornithologist John Gould examined the specimens in early 1837, that the truth emerged: all of them were finches, thirteen closely related species found nowhere else on Earth. Each species had a different beak — some thick and crushing for seeds, some slender and probing for insects, some long and curved for cactus flowers. This pattern, an ancestral species radiating into many specialized forms across nearby islands, would eventually be called adaptive radiation. The Galápagos finches did not give Darwin his theory in a flash of insight on the islands themselves. That popular story is a later invention. But they became, decades after his death, the single most famous illustration of how natural selection shapes living things. Today schoolchildren around the world learn evolution through Darwin's finches, even though Darwin himself wrote very little about them in Origin of Species. This lesson follows the birds from messy field collection to textbook icon.",
